## The short version

Write your project's **design document** (roughly 3–6 pages + diagrams): system context, architecture and major components, data model, interface sketches, technology choices argued against real alternatives, risks and open questions, and an implementation plan. The Wednesday workshop produces most of it at the whiteboard; the graded version is the revised doc a week later.

---

## Why this assignment exists

Code is a terrible medium for making design decisions — by the time a decision is expressed in code, changing it is expensive. A design doc is where you make the expensive choices *while they're still cheap*: what the components are, where state lives, what talks to what, and — the heart of the genre — **why this design beats the alternatives you considered.** As the Google design-doc reading puts it, our job isn't to produce code; it's to solve problems, and prose-plus-diagrams is often the sharpest tool early.

Two habits trained here will follow you everywhere. First, **arguing with alternatives**: "we chose Postgres" is an assertion; "we chose Postgres over SQLite because two of our flows need concurrent writes" is engineering. Design reviews at every serious company run on exactly this move. Second, **writing for the person who isn't in the room**: this doc is graded through the eyes of a future contributor who joins in January and asks "where's the design doc?" — the course's contributor-ready thread, applied to your thinking itself. And practically: a real design doc makes Weeks 9–13 dramatically easier, because decomposition (CP8) falls straight out of a good component diagram.

---

## What to produce

A 3–6 page document, written to the **<course-link type="page" id="pg-design-doc-template">Design Document Template</course-link>** and kept in the repo (it becomes `docs/architecture.md`'s foundation), containing:

1. **Context & scope** — the landscape in a paragraph: what's being built, for whom (link your CP2/proposal; don't repeat them).
2. **Goals & non-goals** — carried from the proposal, now bounding the *design*.
3. **System-context diagram** — the system as one box; every user role and external service around it; labeled arrows.
4. **Architecture & components** — 3–6 components, each with a one-paragraph responsibility and rough interface; the component diagram; one **core user flow traced end-to-end** (your MVP's vertical slice).
5. **Data model** — entities, key fields, relationships. MVP entities solid; roadmap entities dashed.
6. **Technology choices & rationale** — each major choice argued against **one real alternative**.
7. **Risks & open questions** — what you're not sure of yet, honestly (including the traced failure case: what happens when the external service is down?).
8. **Implementation plan** — the build order at milestone altitude (this seeds CP8).

---

## Requirements & constraints

- **Use the template.** Keep every heading, in order — a section that genuinely doesn't apply gets one sentence saying why. Organizations standardize design docs so reviewers know where to look; conforming is part of the job.
- **Team artifact** (solo at solo scope), committed via your own PR workflow.
- **The draft ({{ DESIGN_DRAFT_DUE_DAY }}, {{ DESIGN_DRAFT_DUE }}) can be rough:** photographed whiteboard diagrams + skeleton prose + two argued decisions is a legitimate draft. The revision is where polish happens — and revision is graded.
- **Right altitude:** decisions and trade-offs in; mechanics out. No copy-pasted schemas, no pseudo-code for CRUD, no pinned library versions.
- **Diagrams must agree with prose.** A component in the text that's missing from the diagram (or vice versa) is the most common defect.
- **AI use:** fine for diagram tooling and prose cleanup; the design decisions and their defense must be the team's.

